IMPLEMENTED TWICE, REVERTED TWICE. WHAT THE REGRESSIONS ESTABLISHED. 2026-10-18
================================================================================

ATTEMPT 1 — canonicalize the STORED args (`$0`, `$1`, … by enclosing-binder position,
applied to `args`/`args_source` before hashing and reifying).

  Probes: all three collapsed to one instance, exactly as intended.
  Regression: std/subgroup/products.b4m:62 —
      claim:    (forall i; …) -> inSubgroup(productUpTo(s, ZERO))
      instance: … inSubgroup(productUpTo($0, ZERO)) …
  The stored arg IS the instance's statement (reified → rebuilt → substituted verbatim
  by Elab). Renaming it renames the thing being proved.

ATTEMPT 2 — canonicalize a hash-only VIEW, store the originals unchanged.

  Regression: std/subgroup.b4m:201 and std/set/finite.b4m:3985 —
      step claims  op(p, inverse(p)) = E
      instantiate  op(g, inverse(g)) = E
  Two DISTINCT instantiations, in different proofs, at genuinely different variables
  (`g` and `p`), collapsed to ONE address. The second proof then received the first's
  statement, which names the first's variable.

THE CONCLUSION, AND IT CORRECTS WHAT I ASSERTED EARLIER.

I claimed two same-position eigenvar instantiations "should be ONE instance" because
their statements are alpha-equivalent. That is wrong, and the regression is the proof.
An instance keyed on an eigenvar is NOT a reusable fact: its statement mentions a
variable bound inside ONE proof, so it can only ever serve that proof. Two proofs need
two FACTS because they need two different STATEMENTS. The eigenvar's name is
load-bearing identity, not noise.

WHAT SURVIVES of the original finding, and it is narrower than I said:

  SAME proof, SAME written instantiation, an unrelated step added above it
  → different address for what is genuinely ONE fact.          <- the real bug
  (drafts/schema-hash-decode/eigenvar-arg-depends-on-the-counter.b4m)

  DIFFERENT proofs, different variables
  → different addresses.                                        <- CORRECT
  (two-proofs-same-position.b4m — which I had mislabelled as a bug)

So the serial is not identity, but the binder is. The fix is to make the eigenvar's
name independent of the COUNTER while still distinguishing distinct binders — i.e.
`(spelling, binder loc)`, which is precisely what `hashEntity` already does for
entities and for the same stated reason ("allocation-ordered, hence dependent on the
demand schedule").

WHY THAT IS NOT A SMALL CHANGE. `BinderInfo` (Walk.zig:73) carries sort, source_sort,
fvar and refined — NO location. `LocalIdent` adds `block`, a BlockOrdinal, which is
itself traversal-ordered and so no better than the counter. Giving a binder a stable
identity means threading its source location from `bindProofVar` through `BinderInfo`,
and `freshNamed` is called from ~a dozen sites (fix binders, unpack witnesses,
emitWeakening's synthetic blocks, the guard projection) each of which would need to
supply one. That is a real refactor, not a patch.

RECOMMENDATION: do it as its own piece of work, with the probe
`eigenvar-arg-depends-on-the-counter.b4m` as the acceptance test and
`two-proofs-same-position.b4m` as the guard against over-collapsing. Both are in this
directory and both check under `--draft`.

COST OF LEAVING IT: one written instantiation can mint two instances when steps are
added above it, so the prover occasionally proves the same thing twice. Wasted work,
not unsoundness — each instance is independently kernel-checked. And the decode work
still needs the replay route (README route A'), since the counter remains in the hash.
